prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a 3D platforming level. The player controls a character that jumps between floating platforms and wins by reaching the golden finish platform.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules inside <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Character Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load the provided character model assets/RobotExpressive.glb. This model is a robot character licensed under CC0 and contains the following animation clips (AnimationClip): Idle, Run, Jump, Fall (the names may differ slightly; iterate through gltf.animations to find clips containing these keywords);
(2) After the character is loaded, scale it to an appropriate size (approximately 0.5-0.8 scale) and place it on the starting platform;
(3) Use THREE.AnimationMixer to manage the character animations. Switch animations according to the character state: play Idle while standing, Run while moving, Jump while ascending, and Fall while descending;
(4) When switching animations, use crossFadeTo for smooth transitions (duration: 0.2 seconds);

[Platform Level Layout]
(5) Use THREE.BoxGeometry to construct 6 floating platforms. Each platform must be a rectangular cuboid with thickness (width 4 x height 0.5 x depth 4);
(6) Platform layout (world coordinates):
  - Platform 0 (start, green): position (0, 0, 0)
  - Platform 1 (green): position (6, 1, 0)
  - Platform 2 (green): position (12, 2.5, 3)
  - Platform 3 (green): position (6, 4, 6)
  - Platform 4 (green): position (0, 5.5, 3)
  - Platform 5 (finish, golden): position (6, 7, 0)
(7) Use 0x44aa44 (green) for the starting platform, 0xffd700 (golden) for the finish platform, and 0x66bb66 (light green) for the intermediate platforms;
(8) Optionally add a subtle shadow effect beneath each platform;

[Character Controls—Third-Person]
(9) The camera follows the character from a third-person perspective. Position the camera above and behind the character at a distance of approximately 8-10 units;
(10) Horizontal mouse movement (or dragging) rotates the camera horizontally around the character. Do not use OrbitControls; implement the camera-follow logic yourself;
(11) Use WASD to control character movement. W moves forward, S moves backward, A moves left, and D moves right. Movement direction must be relative to the camera's orientation (that is, W must always move toward the front of the screen rather than along the world-coordinate Z-axis);
(12) The character's movement speed is 5 m/s, and the character must face the movement direction (turn automatically);

[Jumping and Gravity]
(13) Press the Space key to jump. The initial jump velocity is v_y = 8 m/s (upward);
(14) The gravitational acceleration is g = 20 m/s² (downward) and must be applied to the character's vertical velocity;
(15) The character may jump only while on a platform (grounded state). Double jumping is prohibited—pressing Space while airborne has no effect;
(16) grounded detection: Cast a Raycaster ray downward from the character's foot position (length approximately 0.3) to detect whether it hits the top surface of any platform. If it hits, set grounded=true; otherwise, set grounded=false;

[Collision and Landing]
(17) While the character is falling, if the ray from the feet detects a platform, stop the character on top of the platform (y = platform top y-coordinate + character half-height), set vertical velocity to zero, and set the character to grounded;
(18) The character must not pass through platforms—if the character collides with a platform from the side, it must be blocked (simple approach: if horizontal overlap with the platform AABB is detected, push the character back);

[Scoring and Death]
(19) When the character stands on the golden finish platform (grounded and on platform 5), increase the score by 1 and display a victory message. Automatically respawn at the start after 3 seconds (or allow the player to continue playing and attempt to reach it again);
(20) When the character falls below y < -10, increase the death count by 1, immediately teleport the character back to the starting platform at (0, 1.5, 0), set it to the grounded state, and set its vertical velocity to zero;

[Visual Effects]
(21) Use a sky-blue gradient for the scene background (or the solid color 0x87CEEB);
(22) Add one DirectionalLight + AmbientLight to illuminate the scene;
(23) Optionally add a translucent abyss plane far below the platforms for visual appeal;

[HUD and State Exposure]
(24) Display the following in a HUD in the upper-left corner:
  - Score (id="score")
  - Death count (id="deaths")
  - Current character grounded state (id="grounded", displaying true/false)
  - Current platform number (id="currentPlatform", 0-5, or -1 when airborne)
(25) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame, containing:
  - playerPos: { x, y, z }  Character position
  - playerVel: { x, y, z }  Character velocity
  - grounded: boolean       Whether the character is on a platform
  - currentPlatform: number  Current platform number (-1 indicates airborne/falling)
  - score: number           Score
  - deaths: number          Death count
  - animState: string       Current animation state ("idle"/"run"/"jump"/"fall")
  - modelLoaded: boolean    Whether the model has finished loading

Output a single HTML file. Place this file in the same directory as the assets/ folder and run it through a local HTTP server (do not open it directly using file://, because GLTFLoader requires an HTTP request to load the .glb file).
